// IPS/UPS: the synchronous core of Russia's Unified Power System with the
// neighbouring systems that run in parallel with it (spec section 28.3;
// research note docs/research/2026-10-18-world-power-grids-top-8.md, section
// "F - IPS/UPS synchronous core"): Russia's first synchronous zone as an
// administrative approximation, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, mainland
// Azerbaijan, Kyrgyzstan, main Tajikistan and Georgia. The notes exclude the
// Far East zone, Kaliningrad, Armenia, Iran, Turkmenistan, Nakhchivan, the
// separately operated Pamir systems, Afghanistan and Mongolia (Russia-linked
// Mongolian systems are a map symbol only, so Mongolia is not selected).
//
// Grid-operation rulings:
// - Russia's first zone by administrative approximation (section F,
//   "Cartographic method"): Natural Earth's 1:50m admin-0 RUS less the Far
//   East zone's subjects inside the bounds (1:10m admin-1 Amur and Sakha
//   (Yakutia); Khabarovsk, Primorye and the Jewish AO lie east of 122 E), the
//   isolated northern Yakutia (within Sakha) and Kaliningrad (an exclusion,
//   and wholly west of the bounds). Chukotka, Magadan, Kamchatka and
//   Sakhalin lie east of the bounds.
// - Norilsk and Taimyr are out: the Norilsk Industrial District's energy
//   system, with the Kureyskaya and Ust-Khantayskaya hydro stations, is
//   isolated from the Unified Energy System (Nornickel, Annual Report 2023,
//   "Energy assets", read 2026-10-25). Natural Earth has no Taimyr admin-1
//   feature, so the exclusion is 1:10m admin-1 Krasnoyarsk Krai north of
//   66.5 N, which takes in the Taimyr district, Norilsk and both hydro
//   stations (Kureyskaya stands at about 66.9 N); generated by
//   tools/region_authoring/selections/ips_ups_norilsk.args in extent mode
//   (authoring judgment on the parallel).
// - Crimea and Sevastopol are out (spec section 28.3, user decision
//   2026-10-25). Natural Earth's admin-0 RUS includes Crimea, so the
//   selection excludes 1:10m admin-1 UKR:Crimea and UKR:Sevastopol, and the
//   canonical extent (crates/infrastructure/authoring/
//   ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron) is pasted verbatim as well (ruling 11).
//   The extractor's ring is identical to it point for point; no land cell
//   centre lies in either raw polygon (checked against the unsimplified
//   1:10m features).
// - Nakhchivan is out (an exclusion: the eight 1:10m admin-1 rayons Babek,
//   Julfa, Kangarli, Nakhchivan, Ordubad, Sadarak, Shahbuz and Sharur), and so
//   is the Pamir (1:10m admin-1 Gorno-Badakhshan). Armenia's Artsvashen
//   exclave inside Azerbaijan is a hole in admin-0 AZE and stays excluded.
// - Areas the notes don't address (spec section 28.3):
//   - Abkhazia is in. Its grid is fed by the Inguri hydro station, shared
//     with Georgia, and by imports from Russia's grid (JAMnews, "Inguri HPP
//     closing down for 3 months, Abkhazia to receive electricity from
//     Russia", 2021-01-21; Chernomorenergo's winter purchases, JAMnews,
//     "Abkhazia to buy Russian electricity just in case from August to
//     November"), and Georgia's grid runs synchronously with Russia's and
//     Azerbaijan's (section F, citing Sakrusenergo).
//   - South Ossetia is in. It is supplied from Russia's grid over the 110 kV
//     Severny Portal-Java line and, since 2021-11-26, a 110 kV backup line
//     from the rebuilt Severny Portal substation (Civil.ge, "Tskhinvali,
//     Moscow Launch Backup Power Line", 2021-11-30).
//   Both lie inside Natural Earth's admin-0 GEO outline.
//
// Geography: tools/region_authoring/selections/ips_ups.args holds the
// extractor arguments: Natural Earth v5.1.2 1:50m admin-0 RUS, BLR, KAZ,
// UZB, AZE, KGZ, TJK and GEO, dissolved (ruling 8), with the 1:10m admin-1
// exclusions above. Regenerate with
//   tr '\n' '\0' < tools/region_authoring/selections/ips_ups.args \
//       | xargs -0 python3 -I tools/region_authoring/natural_earth.py
// which reports 4 land and 5 exclude rings. The land rings are the
// mainland, southern Novaya Zemlya (Arkhangelsk Oblast) and Kolguyev (Nenets
// AO), both in by the administrative approximation, and Crimea (under its
// exclusions). The selection's exclusions dissolve into 31 rings, of which
// 5 meet the bounds: Crimea with Sevastopol, Sakha with Amur, the Pamir,
// Nakhchivan and the Artsvashen hole; all 5 come out (ruling 9). The
// Norilsk run reports 0 land and 2 exclude rings, and its selection meets
// the bounds in 2 rings (the Krasnoyarsk mainland and a small ring at
// 83.1-83.5 E, 70.4-70.8 N), so both come out.
// Tolerance: 0.02 degrees in both runs, not the default half cell (0.25),
// the same as the canonical Crimea extent. At 0.25 the Artsvashen hole
// collapses (the ring count fails), three Sakha cell centres stay playable
// and land cell centres fall in Ukraine (2), Mongolia (3), China (3),
// Turkmenistan (2) and Armenia (1); at 0.03 one Sakha and one Estonian
// centre remain. At 0.02 no land cell centre lies in a raw excluded 1:10m
// feature (Crimea, Sevastopol, Sakha, Amur, Gorno-Badakhshan, the Nakhchivan
// rayons, Krasnoyarsk north of 66.5 N) or in a raw 1:50m admin-0 neighbour
// (Armenia, Iran, Turkmenistan, Afghanistan, Mongolia, China, Ukraine,
// Turkey, Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland and Norway).
// Europe-Maghreb seam: the borders with Ukraine, Moldova, Latvia, Lithuania,
// Estonia, Poland and Turkey are Natural Earth 1:50m admin-0 shared
// boundaries simplified at 0.02 degrees, so Task 9 can simplify the same
// lines. No nudge rings are needed: every city's cell centre is land.
// Points are (lon, lat) in degrees.
//
// Grid: 23 to 122 E and 37 to 72 N on 99 x 70 cells (1.0 x 0.5 degrees). At
// the middle latitude, 54.5 N, a cell is 64.57 km east-west by 55.60 km
// north-south (aspect 1.161, cell_km 59.917).
//
// economic_zones omitted by authoring choice: the opt-in test needs sourced
// whole-project figures for one same technology in both Russia and
// Kazakhstan or Uzbekistan, and the research note has Russian whole-project
// figures only for coal (P7-IPS-004) and CCGT (P7-IPS-006), while wind has
// only IRENA country market averages in all three countries (P7-IPS-007,
// P7-IPS-013, P7-IPS-014) and one dated 2019 Russian bid (P7-IPS-008). (The
// note's "IPS/UPS economic zones: decision" section; FX rows P7-FX-017 to
// P7-FX-019 exist if a later edition opts in.) Every city uses RUB.
//
// Money is in RUB (roubles) at local price levels from the Plan 7 research
// note (docs/research/2026-10-25-plan-7-regional-economics.md), converted to
// game values by its pacing method. Each entry's comment cites its rows and
// shows the arithmetic; the ratios use full precision (ruling 25).
(
    region_schema: 4,
    id: "ips_ups",
    system_zone: 1,
    // Moscow time (MSK, UTC+3, which Belarus also keeps) is the dispatcher's
    // clock. The other zones are named by their UTC offsets: +4 (Samara,
    // Saratov, Azerbaijan and Georgia), +5 (Yekaterinburg, the Urals,
    // Kazakhstan, which has kept UTC+5 nationwide since March 2024,
    // Uzbekistan and Tajikistan), +6 (Omsk and Kyrgyzstan), +7 (Novosibirsk,
    // Krasnoyarsk and Kuzbass), +8 (Irkutsk and Buryatia) and +9
    // (Zabaykalsky Krai). None observes daylight saving (spec section 28.4).
    time_zones: [
        (id: 1, name_key: "zone-msk", standard_offset_minutes: 180, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 2, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-4", standard_offset_minutes: 240, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 3, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-5", standard_offset_minutes: 300, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 4, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-6", standard_offset_minutes: 360, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 5, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-7", standard_offset_minutes: 420, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 6, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-8", standard_offset_minutes: 480, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
        (id: 7, name_key: "zone-utc-plus-9", standard_offset_minutes: 540, dst_months: [false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false, false]),
    ],
    // Northern meteorological seasons (spec section 28.6).
    calendar: (seasons: [Winter, Winter, Spring, Spring, Spring, Summer, Summer, Summer, Autumn, Autumn, Autumn, Winter]),
    currency: RUB,
    atlas: (colour: "#7652B2"),
    name_key: "region-ips-ups",
    bounds: (west: 23.0, east: 122.0, south: 37.0, north: 72.0),
    grid: (width: 99, height: 70),
    // Generated rings (spec section 28.11.1), each sidecar written by its selection in
    // tools/region_authoring/selections, whose header records the source data and command:
    // natural_earth.ron (ips_ups.args), norilsk.ron (ips_ups_norilsk.args, Norilsk and Taimyr)
    // and the canonical Crimea and Sevastopol extent, ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron
    // (ukraine_ips_operating_extent.args, ruling 11).
    geography: ["natural_earth.ron", "norilsk.ron", "ukraine_ips_operating_extent.ron"],
    land: [],
    exclude: [],
    // Relief: the Greater Caucasus, the Urals, the Altai and Sayan, the
    // ranges around Lake Baikal and in Transbaikalia, the Tian Shan, the
    // Pamir-Alay and the Khibiny on the Kola Peninsula; the West Siberian
    // plain and the Caspian lowland are low. Named ranges only; heights are
    // an authoring judgment (no elevation dataset was used).
    height: (
        base: 0.12,
        blobs: [
            // The Greater Caucasus.
            (lon: 43.5, lat: 42.8, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.5),
            // The Urals, north and south.
            (lon: 59.5, lat: 58.5, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.2),
            (lon: 59.0, lat: 54.0, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.18),
            // The Altai and the Sayan.
            (lon: 87.0, lat: 50.0, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.5),
            (lon: 93.0, lat: 52.3, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.45),
            // Around Lake Baikal and in Transbaikalia.
            (lon: 108.0, lat: 53.0, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.35),
            (lon: 114.0, lat: 51.5, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.3),
            // The Tian Shan and the Pamir-Alay.
            (lon: 77.0, lat: 42.2, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.55),
            (lon: 70.0, lat: 39.3, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.5),
            // The Khibiny.
            (lon: 33.7, lat: 67.7, radius_deg: 0.7, value: 0.2),
            // The West Siberian plain and the Caspian lowland.
            (lon: 73.0, lat: 60.0, radius_deg: 7.0, value: -0.08),
            (lon: 49.0, lat: 47.0, radius_deg: 3.0, value: -0.1),
        ],
    ),
    // Sunshine as site quality without latitude (spec section 28.5), from
    // the annual sunshine of each city's English Wikipedia "Climate data"
    // table (read 2026-10-25): Moscow 1,731 hours (VVC, normals 1961-1990,
    // citing thermograph.ru and Pogoda.ru.net), and from Pogoda.ru.net's
    // 1991-2020 normals Murmansk 1,388.9, Novosibirsk 2,041, Irkutsk 2,301.6,
    // Rostov-on-Don 2,122, Astrakhan 2,407, Tashkent 2,755.6 and Almaty
    // 2,278.2. Each annual total is a share of the possible hours at its
    // latitude (FAO-56 equations 24, 25 and 34 on the 15th of each month),
    // turned into irradiance by the Angstrom-Prescott relation (FAO-56
    // equation 35, a = 0.25, b = 0.50) and scaled as in the
    // India-Nepal-Bhutan file (0.55 for Delhi's 0.5665): Moscow 0.4475 gives
    // the base, 0.435; Murmansk 0.397, Novosibirsk 0.469, Irkutsk 0.498,
    // Rostov-on-Don 0.478, Astrakhan 0.509, Tashkent 0.548 and Almaty 0.495
    // set the blobs (authoring judgment on placement and radius; no
    // irradiance atlas was read).
    irradiance: (
        base: 0.435,
        blobs: [
            // The Kola Peninsula.
            (lon: 33.5, lat: 68.5, radius_deg: 2.0, value: -0.04),
            // The lower Volga and the Caspian lowland, and the Don and Kuban.
            (lon: 47.5, lat: 47.0, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.075),
            (lon: 39.5, lat: 46.5, radius_deg: 2.0, value: 0.04),
            // Novosibirsk and the Altai foothills.
            (lon: 83.0, lat: 54.0, radius_deg: 3.0, value: 0.035),
            // Irkutsk, Lake Baikal and Transbaikalia.
            (lon: 106.0, lat: 52.3, radius_deg: 4.0, value: 0.063),
            // Central Asia: Tashkent and the Fergana valley, and Almaty.
            (lon: 69.3, lat: 41.0, radius_deg: 2.5, value: 0.113),
            (lon: 77.0, lat: 43.5, radius_deg: 1.5, value: 0.055),
        ],
    ),
    // Water for thermal plants: the Baltic, Barents, Black, Azov and Caspian
    // coasts; the Volga, Oka, Kama, Don, Dnieper, Ob, Irtysh, Tom, Yenisei,
    // Angara, Selenga, Ural, Syr Darya, Amu Darya, Chirchiq, Ili and Kura;
    // Lakes Ladoga, Baikal and Balkhash; and the lakes and reservoirs at the
    // Kalinin (Udomlya), Kashira (the Oka) and Cherepet (the Cherepet
    // reservoir) plants. Blobs sit on the Natural Earth coastline and the
    // named rivers (authoring judgment; no water-availability dataset was
    // used).
    cooling_water: (
        base: 0.08,
        blobs: [
            // Coasts: the Gulf of Finland, the Kola Bay, the Black Sea, the
            // Sea of Azov and the Caspian.
            (lon: 29.5, lat: 59.85, radius_deg: 0.6, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 28.2, lat: 59.4, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 33.1, lat: 69.0, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 37.8, lat: 44.7, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 39.7, lat: 43.6, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 41.7, lat: 42.15, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 38.9, lat: 47.2, radius_deg: 0.6, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 47.5, lat: 43.0, radius_deg: 0.6, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 49.9, lat: 40.4, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.7),
            // Lake Ladoga.
            (lon: 31.5, lat: 60.8, radius_deg: 0.7, value: 0.6),
            // The Volga at Tver, Konakovo, Rybinsk, Yaroslavl, Kostroma,
            // Nizhny Novgorod, Kazan, Samara, Balakovo, Saratov, Volgograd and
            // Astrakhan.
            (lon: 35.9, lat: 56.86, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 36.77, lat: 56.74, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 38.82, lat: 58.08, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 39.89, lat: 57.63, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 41.17, lat: 57.46, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 44.0, lat: 56.33, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 49.1, lat: 55.8, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 50.1, lat: 53.2, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 47.8, lat: 52.05, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 46.03, lat: 51.53, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 44.6, lat: 48.75, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 48.03, lat: 46.35, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            // The Kalinin plant's lakes at Udomlya, the Oka at Kaluga and
            // Kashira, the Cherepet reservoir and the Moskva below Moscow.
            (lon: 35.06, lat: 57.91, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 36.27, lat: 54.53, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 38.26, lat: 54.86, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 36.48, lat: 54.14, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 38.75, lat: 55.1, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Desna at Desnogorsk, the Seym at Kurchatov and the Don at
            // Novovoronezh, Rostov-on-Don and Volgodonsk.
            (lon: 33.28, lat: 54.15, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 35.6, lat: 51.68, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 39.2, lat: 51.27, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 39.7, lat: 47.25, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 42.4, lat: 47.6, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Dnieper and the Viliya in Belarus.
            (lon: 30.3, lat: 53.9, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 26.1, lat: 54.76, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Kama at Perm, Votkinsk and Nizhnekamsk, the Belaya at Ufa
            // and the Ural at Orenburg.
            (lon: 56.3, lat: 58.0, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 54.08, lat: 56.79, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 52.28, lat: 55.7, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 55.95, lat: 54.73, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 55.1, lat: 51.77, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Beloyarsk reservoir and the Iset, and Chelyabinsk's lakes.
            (lon: 61.32, lat: 56.84, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 61.4, lat: 55.15, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Ob at Surgut, Novosibirsk and Barnaul; the Irtysh at Omsk,
            // Pavlodar and Oskemen; the Tom at Tomsk and Kemerovo.
            (lon: 73.4, lat: 61.25, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 82.95, lat: 55.0, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 83.78, lat: 53.35, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 73.37, lat: 54.98, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 76.95, lat: 52.29, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 82.6, lat: 49.95, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 84.95, lat: 56.5, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 86.06, lat: 55.36, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Yenisei at Krasnoyarsk and the Sayano-Shushenskaya
            // reservoir; the Angara at Irkutsk, Bratsk and Ust-Ilimsk; Lake
            // Baikal; the Selenga at Ulan-Ude; Lake Kenon at Chita.
            (lon: 92.87, lat: 56.0, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 91.4, lat: 52.8, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 104.3, lat: 52.3, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 101.8, lat: 56.3, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 102.7, lat: 58.0, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 107.5, lat: 53.0, radius_deg: 0.8, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 107.6, lat: 51.83, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 113.4, lat: 52.05, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            // The Syr Darya, the Chirchiq at Tashkent, the Amu Darya in
            // Khorezm, the Ili at Kapchagay, Lake Balkhash, the Irtysh-Karaganda
            // canal at Ekibastuz and the Kura at Mingachevir and Tbilisi.
            (lon: 68.7, lat: 40.85, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 66.0, lat: 44.85, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 69.5, lat: 41.4, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 60.6, lat: 41.5, radius_deg: 0.5, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 77.1, lat: 43.9, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 74.9, lat: 46.8, radius_deg: 0.8, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 75.3, lat: 51.7, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 47.05, lat: 40.77, radius_deg: 0.4, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 44.8, lat: 41.7, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.6),
        ],
    ),
    // Hydro sites at real dams and stations (each plant's Wikidata P625,
    // with its installed capacity from P2109, read 2026-10-25):
    // Sayano-Shushenskaya (Q474551, 6,400 MW), Krasnoyarsk (Q1120899, 6,000),
    // Bratsk (Q1965517, 4,500), Ust-Ilimsk (Q2719580, 3,840), Boguchany
    // (Q890717, 2,997), Irkutsk (Q1965660, 662.4), Novosibirsk (Q4325430,
    // 475), the Volga station at Volgograd (Q1743883, 2,660.5), Zhiguli
    // (Q1965688, 2,404), Saratov (Q1542516, 1,403.78), Cheboksary (Q2270738,
    // 1,404), Nizhnekamsk (Q12133686, 1,205), Votkinsk (Q1965109, 1,035),
    // Rybinsk (Q1964590, 386.4), Chirkey (Q117578, 1,000), Enguri (Q162887,
    // 1,300), Nurek (Q913334, 3,000), Rogun (Q1891844, 3,780), Toktogul
    // (Q278196, 1,200), Bukhtarma (Q1046946, 675) and Shulbinsk (Q4527522,
    // 702). Values rank them by that capacity (authoring judgment).
    hydro_potential: (
        base: 0.0,
        blobs: [
            (lon: 91.37013056, lat: 52.82526944, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.95),
            (lon: 92.294444, lat: 55.934722, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.95),
            (lon: 101.786111, lat: 56.286111, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.9),
            (lon: 102.693611, lat: 57.967778, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.9),
            (lon: 99.15833333, lat: 58.7, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.85),
            (lon: 104.32861111, lat: 52.24277778, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.65),
            (lon: 82.9863889, lat: 54.85027779, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.6),
            (lon: 44.67194444, lat: 48.82611111, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.85),
            (lon: 49.47888889, lat: 53.42666667, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.85),
            (lon: 47.754944, lat: 52.053028, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.75),
            (lon: 47.46555556, lat: 56.13805556, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.75),
            (lon: 52.27833333, lat: 55.69944444, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 54.08472222, lat: 56.79138889, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 38.824908, lat: 58.082117, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 46.87138889, lat: 42.97722222, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.7),
            (lon: 42.03194444, lat: 42.75916667, radius_deg: 0.3, value: 0.75),
            (lon: 69.34823, lat: 38.37121, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.85),
            (lon: 69.7725, lat: 38.6825, radius_deg: 0.35, value: 0.85),
